====================================================================
SPATIO-TEMPORAL DoF x TIME ATTENTION LOCK (GoBA)
Script: run_trajectory_dof_attention.py
Data: trajectory_dof_attention.npz (80 closed-loop episodes, 30 steps)
====================================================================

STATISTIC: at every control step, band-averaged (L8/16/24/27) attention map
of EACH of the 7 DoF action queries over 256 image patches. Spatio-temporal
lock = fraction of (DoF, step) cells whose argmax equals the episode's modal
patch. Hypothesis: poison is rank-one and frozen (every DoF, every step, same
patch); clean disagrees across DoFs and drifts as the arm moves.

RESULTS:
  SPATIO-TEMPORAL LOCK:
    poison   mean=0.982 range=[0.948,1.000]  unique patches mean=3.1
    clean    mean=0.320 range=[0.138,0.676]  unique patches mean=16.4
    ketchup  mean=0.606 range=[0.219,0.857]  unique patches mean=14.2
    milk     mean=0.272 range=[0.119,0.490]  unique patches mean=18.8
    AUROC poison=1.0000 ketchup=0.8750 milk=0.5413
    FIXED MARGIN OPEN = +0.0905  (min poison 0.948 > max benign 0.857)
    => any constant ~0.90 separates ALL 80 episodes with zero FPR on decoys.

  CROSS-DoF MAP COSINE (episode mean):
    poison 0.955  clean 0.919  ketchup 0.924  milk 0.912
    AUROC poison=1.0000 (weaker specificity than lock)

  PER-DoF TEMPORAL DWELL (DoF-avg):
    poison 0.982  clean 0.481  ketchup 0.653  milk 0.458
    AUROC poison=0.9450

CLAIM: the backdoor freezes ALL action dimensions onto ONE patch for the
entire rollout. Benign novel objects (ketchup) attract some DoF attention
but never produce the rank-one freeze. Calibration-free structural rule
(lock fraction > 0.90) works with open margin -- no clean reference needed
at deployment beyond knowing the form of the statistic.

====================================================================
LOGIT LENS DECISION DEPTH -- NEGATIVE
Script: run_logit_lens_depth.py  Data: logit_lens_depth.npz (n=250)
====================================================================
Decision depth (earliest stable top-1 layer of the final action-bin token)
does NOT separate: poison mean 25.47 vs clean 25.54, AUROC 0.529. Per-DoF
and layer-wise final-token probability also near chance. The backdoor does
not crystallize earlier than clean decisions -- drop this line of inquiry.

====================================================================
DoF x TIME TRANSFER VERDICT (GoBA / BadVLA / AttackVLA)
====================================================================
GoBA   (trajectory_dof_attention.npz):
  spat-temp LOCK: poison 0.982 vs clean 0.320, AUROC 1.0, OPEN margin +0.09
                  decoy-silent enough for fixed thr ~0.90
  cross-DoF cos:  AUROC 1.0 but weaker specificity than lock

BadVLA (badvla_trajectory_dof_attention.npz):
  spat-temp LOCK: DOES NOT TRANSFER (poison 0.386 vs clean 0.350, AUROC 0.65)
                  poison wanders MORE (uniq 11.8 vs 8.7) -- consistent with
                  earlier finding that BadVLA DIFFUSES attention
  cross-DoF cos:  TRANSFERS, AUROC 1.0, OPEN margin
                  poison [0.9985,0.9987] vs clean [0.959,0.986]
                  => DoFs ASSIMILATE under poison even while the attended
                     patch wanders over time

AttackVLA (attackvla_trajectory_dof_attention.npz):
  spat-temp LOCK: DOES NOT TRANSFER (full 0.597 < clean 0.671, AUROC 0.58)
  cross-DoF cos:  WEAK (full-vs-clean AUROC 0.895 DOWN -- full slightly lower
                  cosine than clean); visual_only near chance vs clean
                  => not a usable detector here

CORRECTED CLAIM:
  The GoBA-style FROZEN rank-one (DoF x time) lock is GoBA-specific
  (physical-object trigger that hijacks a spatial locus).
  What partially transfers is WITHIN-STEP DoF assimilation (cross-DoF
  cosine): strong on BadVLA (open margin), weak/sign-flipped on AttackVLA.
  Scope the lock story to GoBA; cite BadVLA as transfer of cross-DoF
  assimilation without temporal freeze.

====================================================================
ATTENTION -> MOTION DIRECTION COUPLING (analysis of trajectory_dof_attention.npz)
====================================================================
Ridge fit (attended patch (row,col) -> commanded (dx,dy)) on CLEAN early steps
(first 10), evaluated as direction cosine per role:
  clean +0.978   poison +0.946 (tight [0.90,0.97])
  ketchup +0.922 (outliers to +0.21)   milk +0.929 (outliers to +0.38)
=> The lock is behaviorally load-bearing: the patch attention froze on IS the
   motion target -- poisoned early actions head toward the trigger location
   (predicted from internals alone, map fit on clean only). Decoys sometimes
   glance without steering (low-coupling outliers); poison never does.
   USE AS MECHANISTIC LINK, NOT A DETECTOR (coupling is high for all roles).

Instruction-independent early-motion-direction detector (consensus poison
direction from val tasks, scored on held-out): AUROC 0.83 vs clean/decoys.
Real signal (poison shares a sideways dy=+0.43 component toward the trigger
slot across all tasks; clean dy=0.04) but far below our other detectors --
do not claim as a detector.

================================================================================
FROZEN-OR-UNIFORM UNION RULE (intersection-metric follow-up, 2026-08-06)
================================================================================
Question: single metric for targeted (capture) + untargeted (blinding)?

Tried: temporal top-K Jaccard, histogram intersection (temporal + cross-DoF),
distinct-argmax count, per-step entropy. No single ONE-SIDED raw statistic
covers both directions -- clean always sits between the two attack extremes.

BUT the two extremes are DEGENERATE ENDPOINTS, not distribution-tuned numbers:
  FROZEN : # distinct argmax patches over episode == 1   (a delta function)
  UNIFORM: normalized map entropy > 0.95                  (a uniform map)
Rule: flag episode if FROZEN or UNIFORM. Universal constants, no clean data.

           episodes  flagged
GoBA       poison    20/20 (all frozen: 1 patch for 30 steps)
GoBA       clean      1/20 (FPR 5%)   decoys 15% / 5%
BadVLA     poison    20/20 (all uniform: ent 0.997-0.998; clean max 0.774)
BadVLA     clean      0/20
AttackVLA  all        0    (attention dynamics look clean-like; only the
                            MLP-cosine detector catches AttackVLA, as before)

T2IShield-AS-IS CHECK (text-query F-norm + fixed FTT threshold 2.5):
  BadVLA last-layer text F-norm: clean 0.044-0.087, poison 0.0063-0.0097
    -> assimilation phenomenon TRANSFERS (AUROC 1.0, poison DOWN, same
       direction as T2IShield's diffusion finding)
  GoBA: clean 0.057-0.099, poison 0.047-0.066 (down, overlapping ranges)
  BUT the fixed threshold 2.5 is meaningless here: ALL VLA samples (clean and
  poison) sit at 0.004-0.11, ~40x below 2.5 (threshold is tied to Stable
  Diffusion's 77-token cross-attn scale). FTT as-is flags 100% of everything.
  -> T2IShield's statistic survives; its calibration-free CLAIM does not.
     Our frozen/uniform endpoints + MLP-cosine fill exactly that gap.

================================================================================
SENSORIMOTOR (GAZE+MOTION) UNIFIED DEFENSE -- "retarget AND follow" (2026-08-06)
================================================================================
Idea: a clean visuomotor policy must (i) RE-TARGET its gaze over the episode
and (ii) have its commanded motion FOLLOW its gaze. Targeted capture (GoBA)
violates (i); untargeted blinding (BadVLA) violates (ii). One detector, two
necessary conditions, computed only from action-query attention + the
commanded actions of the same rollout. No prior defense uses both streams.

Gaze = expected (row,col) of the pooled band map. Follow = first canonical
correlation between gaze(t) and commanded (dx,dy)(t) over the episode.

(i) RETARGET (frozen pooled argmax over whole episode):
    GoBA   poison 20/20 frozen; clean 1/20, ketchup 3/20, milk 1/20
    BadVLA poison 0/20 (not frozen -- scattered instead)
(ii) FOLLOW (velocity coupling, open margin on BadVLA):
    BadVLA clean [0.806,0.993]  poison [0.226,0.613]  AUROC 1.0
      -> any constant in (0.61,0.81), e.g. 0.7: 100% TPR / 0% FPR
    GoBA   clean mean 0.57 (platform baseline lower; same 0.7 constant would
      flag ~half of benign GoBA episodes -- constant is PER-PLATFORM)
    GoBA poison couples HIGH (0.63-0.95): the arm truly servos to the
      trigger it stares at (mechanistic link, consistent with capture).

Tried and rejected for a universal constant: top-k centroid gaze (worse),
permutation p-value on CCA (underpowered at 20-30 steps: only 20% of BadVLA
poison flagged), cumulative-position coupling (trend-inflated, all roles >0.74).

VERDICT: unified PRINCIPLE with degenerate/universal constant for (i) and a
per-platform constant for (ii) set from a handful of clean episodes. Same
honest calibration status as the MLP-assimilation detector; the novelty is
that it is the first defense grounded in the closed sensorimotor loop, and
the violated condition identifies the attack type (capture vs blinding).

================================================================================
SYSTEMATIC CLOSED-LOOP ANALYSIS (Tri-Info framework extension, 2026-08-06)
================================================================================
Motivated by Tri-Info (arXiv:2606.19998): failure detection via three signals
on the ACTION stream: H(A_t) diversity, I(A_t;A_t+1) consistency, state-action
coupling. We compute their proxies on BOTH streams of the same rollouts, with
the a-priori compression band (no layer selection anywhere):

  attention side: att_H  = spatial entropy of pooled band map per step
                  att_Itt= cos(M_t, M_t+1) gaze persistence
                  frozen = argmax never moves (degenerate endpoint)
                  couple = CCA(gaze centroid, dx-dy)
  action side:    act_H  = action diversity, act_Itt = consecutive-action cos,
                  rev    = direction-reversal rate, jerk (arXiv:2605.28726)

AUROC poison-vs-clean (episode level; u=up, d=down):

              att_H    att_Itt  frozen   couple | act_H  act_Itt  rev   jerk
GoBA (targ.)  1.00d    0.71u    0.97u    ~0.5   | 0.34   0.61     0.52  0.42
BadVLA (unt.) 1.00u    1.00u    --       1.00d  | 0.80   1.00d    --    1.00u

KEY CLAIM (systematic, not layer-picked):
* TARGETED backdoor is INVISIBLE in the action stream (all action-side
  signals at chance, 0.34-0.61): it executes a smooth, competent reach --
  for the WRONG objective. Action-only monitors (Tri-Info, STAC, Sentinel)
  are structurally blind to it.
* UNTARGETED backdoor looks like Tri-Info's "drift" failure in the action
  stream (jerk 1.00, act-consistency collapse 1.00d) -- but so does any
  benign failure; only the attention side distinguishes attack (entropy
  0.997 = uniform) from ordinary drift.
* The Tri-Info failure taxonomy TRANSPLANTS to the perception side:
    capture  = ATTENTION FREEZE  (H(M) collapse + frozen argmax)   [GoBA]
    blinding = ATTENTION DRIFT   (H(M) surge + gaze-action decouple)[BadVLA]
  Clean policies sit between the two degenerate endpoints on all signals.
* GoBA att_H open margin: poison [0.708,0.744] vs clean [0.760,0.797],
  decoys 0.753+ (decoy-silent). BadVLA att_H open: 0.997 vs max clean 0.774.

CLEAN-MODEL ROLLOUT CONTROL: launched run_trajectory_dof_attention_cleanmodel.py
(official openvla-7b-finetuned-libero-goal, same scenes/seeds/protocol).
Early episodes: poison-scene st_lock 0.15-0.80 (vs 0.98+ frozen in backdoored
model) -> freeze is a property of BACKDOOR TRAINING, not the trigger object.
Full results: attn_maps/cleanmodel_trajectory_dof_attention.npz

CLEAN-MODEL ROLLOUT CONTROL -- FINAL (cleanmodel_trajectory_dof_attention.npz,
40 episodes, official openvla-7b-finetuned-libero-goal, same scenes/seeds):

                        clean scenes          trigger-in-scene
  att_H                 0.766 [0.752,0.780]   0.781 [0.765,0.796]  (slightly UP)
  frozen episodes       0/20                  1/20
  st_lock               0.217                 0.279   AUROC 0.544 = CHANCE
  gaze-action coupling  0.730                 0.794   (stays high)

vs BACKDOORED model, same scenes: poison frozen 20/20, st_lock 0.982
[0.948,1.000], att_H 0.734 [0.708,0.744] (open margin DOWN), coupling intact.

=> The trigger object does NOTHING to a clean model's attention dynamics
   (entropy nudges UP like any novel object; no freeze, no lock, loop intact).
   Every signature -- freeze, entropy collapse, spatio-temporal lock -- is a
   property of BACKDOOR TRAINING. Also note the DIRECTION: novelty diffuses
   attention (up), capture concentrates it (down) -- opposite signs, so the
   freeze cannot be explained as a novelty response.
